<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khiva</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khiva is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, long the capital of the Khanate of Khiva. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> campaigned against it in 1740 during his Central Asian expeditions. In the nineteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> reduced Khiva to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> as part of its expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khiva.jpg" alt="Khiva" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fulvio Spada from Torino, Italy · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_the_city_walls,_Khiva_(4934484894).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Khiva is a city of about 93,000 people in Khorazm Region, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. According to archaeological data it was established around 2,500 years ago, and in 1997 it marked its 2,500th anniversary. It is the former capital of Khwarezmia, the Khanate of Khiva and the Khorezm People's Soviet Republic. Its inner town, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itchan-kala">Itchan Kala</a>, was the first site in Uzbekistan and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to be inscribed on the World Heritage List, in 1991. The scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-biruni">Al-Biruni</a> (973–1048) was born in either Khiva or the nearby city of Kath. The origin of its name is unknown, and a traditional story attributes the city to Shem, a son of Noah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Eastern Iranian language Khwarezmian was spoken in Khiva in its early history. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> replaced the Iranian ruling class in the 10th century, and the region gradually came to have a majority of Turkic speakers. The earliest records of the city are in Muslim travel accounts of the 10th century, although archaeology shows habitation in the 6th century. By the early 17th century Khiva was the capital of the Khanate of Khiva, ruled by the Uzbek Kongirad dynasty.</p><p>From the 17th century Khiva developed as a slave market, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> it was known for centuries as a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. In the first half of the 19th century about 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and an unknown number of Russians were enslaved there before being sold, and many were put to work on the construction of buildings in the walled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itchan-kala">Itchan Kala</a>.</p><p>In 1873 the Russian general Konstantin von Kaufman attacked Khiva, which fell on 28 May. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> controlled the khanate, but it let Khiva remain a nominally quasi-independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. The last khan of the ruling dynasty was removed in 1919, and Khiva became the capital of the short-lived Khorezm People's Soviet Republic (1920–1924), which was incorporated into the USSR in 1924.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Khiva is split into two parts. The inner town, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itchan-kala">Itchan Kala</a>, is encircled by brick walls, with foundations believed to date to the 10th century and present walls from the late 17th century, up to 10 metres high. It retains more than 50 historic monuments and 250 old houses, mostly of the 18th or 19th century. The Juma Mosque was rebuilt in 1788–89 and its hypostyle hall keeps 112 columns taken from older buildings. The blue tower of Kalta Minor was begun in 1851 by Mohammed Amin Khan and left unfinished when he died.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khiva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1740 he campaigned in Central Asia, subduing Bukhara and <b>Khiva</b>, while keeping up military pressure on the Ottomans in the west, which ended in his victory at Kars in 1745.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The subjugation of the Central Asian khanates of Bukhara (1868), <b>Khiva</b> (1873) and Kokand (1876) brought the frontier to the borders of Afghanistan and British India, and generated the 'Great Game' rivalry with Britain that governed Central Asian political geography until the Anglo-Russian Convention of 1907.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Uzbek tribes from north of the Aral Sea invaded in 1501, and before the Russians arrived the country was divided between the Emirate of Bukhara and the khanates of <b>Khiva</b> and Kokand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Wars with the Khanate of Kokand and <b>Khiva</b> were frequent.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-bukhara">Emirate of Bukhara</a></li><li><blockquote>The juz was nomadic, raiding and trading with Russia and <b>Khiva</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/junior-juz">Junior Juz</a></li><li><blockquote>The Arabshahid dynasty ruled at first from Urgench and later from <b>Khiva</b>, which became the capital in the 17th century as the Amu Darya changed course.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-khiva">Khanate of Khiva</a></li></ul></section>
